/*  A local may not re-declare a name an enclosing block still has open.

    I6 has no block scope: every local is hoisted to the routine header, one slot per name. So the
    inner declaration would not be a second variable — it would write through to the outer one, and
    the outer would keep the inner's value after the block closed (`int z = 1; if(c){ int z = 2; }
    print(z)` gave 2, where C, C# and TypeScript give 1). Sibling blocks may still reuse a name:
    run_sibling_block_locals.bgl.  */
// EXPECT_ERROR: already declared in an enclosing block
#beguilerSettings { target = z5; informName = "none"; }
void main(){
    bool a = true;
    int z = 1;
    if(a){ int z = 2; print(z); }
    print(z);
}
